WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.560
In this case, "x" gets converted to a boolean
truth value to decide which way the "if" goes.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.740
It’s up to the language to decide how this
conversion happens, so you can end up with

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.040
contradictory results like this.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:15.749
In Python 'if "":' will give you false.

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:21.190
But in C++ or in C, 'if ("")' will give you
true.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:25.320
Effective programmers need to know the rules
of their language, so let’s take a look

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:31.140
at the rules for Python, C++ and C. As a bonus,
we’ll get to see some of the inner workings

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.590
of how CPython is written.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:39.830
In Python, the rule is that literal True is
true, literal False and None are false, a

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:46.970
number-like object is false if it represents
some kind of zero, and a container is false

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:50.500
if it represents something that's empty.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.090
Everything else by default is true.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:58.120
This explains why Python thinks "" is false,
because it's like an empty container of characters.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:03.640
There are actually 2 ways that a class can
opt-out of the default true conversion though,

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.140
and a lot of you might not know the second
one.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.960
The first way is the obvious one, just define
a "__bool__" magic method of the class and

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.930
put in your own logic.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.250
The second way is by defining a "__len__"
magic method.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:20.409
Basically, if you define a length for your
class, Python assumes it’s a container.

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:25.310
So if you return zero you get false, and anything
else you get true.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:28.210
But how do I know that this is the way that
it works?

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:32.360
The rule is buried somewhere in the documentation,
but I saw the audience drop-off the last time

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.780
I tried to show docs on screen, so I found
a better way.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.310
In the modern day we have Compiler Explorer
to show us where to look.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:44.049
Yes, Compiler Explorer works with Python,
and a number of other languages, it just shows

00:01:44.049 --> 00:01:47.790
you bytecode instead of assembly,
though for some reason it only supports up

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.650
to 3.8 (hint hint).

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:55.210
When I put a basic "if x:" into Compiler Explorer,
it shows me the bytecode instruction to look

00:01:55.210 --> 00:02:00.130
at is called POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE,
so I just need to look in Python’s source

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.799
for what that code does.

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:07.210
This file is "ceval.c" in CPython’s source.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:14.770
Here we see how POP_JUMP_IF_FALSE is handled:
it checks for literal True, and literal False,

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.700
doing what it's supposed to do in either of
those cases,

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.841
and then otherwise it calls this function
"PyObject_IsTrue" to determine whether or

00:02:22.841 --> 00:02:25.950
not the object is true or false.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:31.420
This function "PyObject_IsTrue" is found in
"object.c", and it's where the bulk of the

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.450
real logic happens.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:38.660
It checks for literal True, False, and None,
and then checks to see if a "__bool__" is

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:42.500
defined, calling it if it exists.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.629
Otherwise it checks in two places if "__len__"
is defined, calling it if it exists, or just

00:02:47.629 --> 00:02:50.980
returning 1 to signal the default true.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.840
Negative numbers here are for errors.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.561
So that’s the final answer for Python, it
uses "__bool__", then "__len__", otherwise

00:02:58.561 --> 00:03:01.810
you get the default true.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.330
What about C++?

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:09.519
For numbers the rule is the same as Python,
zeroes are false, and everything else is true.

00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:14.900
Arrays are treated like pointers to their
first element, and pointers are treated like

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:20.099
numbers, so a null pointer is false and all
the other pointers are true.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:27.659
That explains why "" literal is true in C++,
because in C++ "" literal is a single element

00:03:27.659 --> 00:03:32.690
character array, which points to some valid
nonzero memory location storing a single null-terminator.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:36.879
It’s not treated like an empty container
like in Python.

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:42.230
In fact, it’s not common in C++ for a container
to have its truth value determined by whether

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:43.300
or not it’s empty.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:49.610
"if (x)" doesn’t even compile for most standard
containers, like std::string or std::vector.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:53.470
But there are some, like std::optional, where
it does compile.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:59.819
By default, if you just define your own class
or struct, "if (x)" is just not going to compile.

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:04.110
But if you want “if (x)” to work, all
you need to do is define an "operator bool()"

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.879
member function.

00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:10.230
Compiler explorer shows us that this "operator
bool()" is the thing that gets called to do

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:14.390
the conversion of "x" into a boolean for the
"if" statement.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:16.199
Pure C on the other hand...

00:04:16.199 --> 00:04:18.590
Well, C doesn’t do stuff for you.

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:23.949
If it’s not a number, pointer, or array,
"if (x)" just isn’t going to compile.

00:04:23.949 --> 00:04:28.400
Define your own function, be explicit and
convert your "x" to a bool yourself.

